=== Transition: Strategies and Mapping <sec-a-pay-transition>

The transition strategies of @sec-a-strategies turn this analysis model into the design model. Eight of the nine change Pay upfront:

- *S1 Language and framework (AD-01, AD-18; NFR-05, 06, 26):* impacted `Payment`, `PaymentGateway` → resultant immutable records `CheckoutPayment`, `CheckoutQuote`, `PaymentSucceeded`, `CheckoutGateway.Session`, `CheckoutGateway.Event`, `PaymentBusinessPort.Outcome`; each state change returns a new record (`succeed`, `bind`). Dynamics @fig-a-pay-ds-settle.
- *S2 Presentation (AD-09; NFR-07, 12, 16, 25):* impacted `PaymentUI` → resultant the frontend payment feature and its receipt page, which poll the receipt (NFR-25); not drawn (see @sec-a-strategies). Dynamics @fig-a-pay-ds-receipt.
- *S3 Distribution (AD-02, AD-06, AD-09; NFR-05, 07, 64, 66):* impacted `PayUpfrontControl`, `BaseUseCaseControl` → resultant `StripeCheckoutService` and `PaymentBrowserService` in the payment module, and the payment-owned ports `PaymentBusinessPort` and `PaymentIntentPort`, which the order module implements (`OrderFulfilmentAdapter`, `PurchaseIntentAppService`). Payment and order stay in commerce-svc because one transaction crosses them (AD-02). The only remote call is the base use case's `UserAppService.requireActive`, which fails closed (AD-06): an identity outage rolls the webhook back with HTTP 500 and Stripe redelivers (NFR-66). The call runs under locks 1, 2 and 4, bounded by a 1 s connect and 2 s read timeout (`application.yml`) inside the 10 s transaction, so another settlement of the same team waits at most that long. Dynamics @fig-a-pay-ds-outcome.
- *S4 Persistence (AD-03, AD-11; NFR-05, 13):* impacted `Payment`, `ProviderReport`, `ReviewCase`, `Refund` → resultant aggregate root `CheckoutPayment` behind `CheckoutPaymentRepository`, implemented by `CheckoutPaymentRepositoryImpl` over `PaymentMapper` (the representative chain, @fig-a-pay-dc-receipt; every other repository follows it). The report is a `log_payment_webhook_event` row and the review case a `biz_payment_review_case` row with `log_payment_review`, both written by that repository. The refund is its own aggregate, so its lifecycle has its own repository (`biz_refund` behind `RefundRepository`, UC6). Only its creation goes through `CheckoutPaymentRepository.requireRefund`, because one SQL statement copies the row from the succeeded payment (`INSERT … SELECT`: amount, currency, intent, team) while that payment is locked (lock 4), inside the settlement transaction; UC6 creates its obligations the same way through `requireRefundsForExpiredTeam` (@sec-a-uc06). Dynamics @fig-a-pay-ds-receipt.
- *S5 External interface (backend ADRs 0001, 0004, 0005, and AD-06 for the settlement's identity call; NFR-21, NFR-22, NFR-23, NFR-31, NFR-50, NFR-66):* impacted `PaymentGateway` → resultant inbound `StripeWebhookController`, port `CheckoutGateway` and adapter `StripeCheckoutGateway` (the only Checkout SDK user; `StripeClientProvider` builds the client), with idempotency key `CheckoutPayment.providerKey()`. Backend ADR 0005 keeps Checkout behind an environment flag. Three rules: no Stripe call inside a database transaction, every report verified and read back, and an unknown outcome goes to an operator. Dynamics @fig-a-pay-ds-bind, @fig-a-pay-ds-webhook.
- *S6 Security (AD-05, AD-09; NFR-01, 46, 47, 58, 68):* impacted `PaymentUI`, `PaymentGateway` → resultant buyer identity from the Sa-Token session, the `X-BlocBuy-Request` header, `RateLimitGateway` (60 preparations a minute per buyer), signature and sandbox checks (`StripeSandbox`) on the public webhook, and a receipt only for its own buyer. Dynamics @fig-a-pay-ds-prepare, @fig-a-pay-ds-webhook.
- *S7 Scheduling (AD-16; NFR-15, 32, 61):* not applied to the payment itself. Lapsed intents are swept by `PurchaseIntentExpiryJob` in the order module, designed with the intent's lifecycle in UC3 (@fig-a-uc03-ds-expiry); the sweep excludes an intent whose payment succeeded (`PaymentIntentExpirySupport.hasSucceededIntent`). `StripeCheckoutService.expireDueSessions` runs only as an operator command, not drawn.
- *S8 Concurrency (AD-02, AD-03; NFR-13, 21, 27, 30):* impacted `PurchaseIntent`, `Payment` → resultant the lock order team (1) → intent (2) → payment (4) in both transactions that write a payment (no order row exists yet, so lock 3 is not taken; receipt and product follow inside fulfilment), and unique indexes `uq_log_payment_webhook_event_event_id`, `uq_biz_payment_request_key`, `uq_biz_payment_pending_intent` and one refund per payment. The settlement transaction spans five aggregates (payment, intent, team, order and `Product`, one unit of stock), as A.2 S8 lists and justifies (@sec-a-strategies): money and seat are atomic, all five live in one database, and a 10 s timeout bounds it. Dynamics @fig-a-pay-ds-settle.
- *S9 Domain and integration events (AD-04; NFR-30, 39, 59, 60, 61, 63):* impacted `Payment` → resultant domain event `PaymentSucceeded`, published through `ApplicationEventPublisher` inside the settlement transaction; at commit (`BEFORE_COMMIT`) the outbox writer turns it into `PaymentSucceededV1` (@sec-a-notifications). Dynamics @fig-a-pay-ds-outcome.

