=== Design Sequence Diagrams <sec-a-pay-ds>

Each design sequence retells part of @tbl-a-pay-flows; the step tags in the notes link the two. A `critical` fragment is one database transaction, labelled with its propagation, isolation and timeout. HTTP requests, including Stripe's webhook, are synchronous calls with a reply; the HTTP status of the reply is what tells Stripe whether to report again.

*Preparation.* A payment page is prepared in two short transactions with the Stripe call between them, never inside one (S5): no row lock is held while Stripe answers. Transaction 1 (@fig-a-pay-ds-prepare) locks the intent through the port (and, for a JOIN only, the team first), checks the attempt and inserts the payment as `CREATED`. The Stripe call (@fig-a-pay-ds-bind) carries the key `blocbuy-checkout-<payment id>`, so a retry within 55 s gets the same page. Transaction 2 takes locks 1 → 2 → 4 in the fixed order and links the page to the payment.

*Settlement.* @fig-a-pay-ds-webhook shows the webhook's arrival: verification, the sandbox check and the read-back all run outside any transaction. @fig-a-pay-ds-settle is the settlement transaction. It claims the event first, so a redelivered report stops there (A11). It then locks the team and the intent through the port (locks 1 and 2) and the payment (lock 4), and checks the page before changing any state. @fig-a-pay-ds-outcome continues the same transaction. `PaymentSucceeded` is published before the expiry check, so the outbox records it on every succeeded payment, whether fulfilled or refunded. If the quote has lapsed, `fulfil` is never called and the outcome is `BUSINESS_EXPIRED` (A14). Otherwise the base use case answers through the port; its side is drawn in @sec-a-uc03 and, for a join, in @fig-a-uc04-ds-fulfil and @fig-a-uc04-ds-admit. A technical failure anywhere rolls back the event claim with everything else (E1), and Stripe's redelivery retries the steps.

*Receipt.* @fig-a-pay-ds-receipt reads the payment once and its refund once (NFR-38), in a read-only snapshot. It also shows the representative persistence chain and the feature switch that answers A1.

