=== State Machines <sec-a-pay-states>

Pay upfront owns the lifecycle of the checkout payment, `CheckoutPayment.Status` (@fig-a-pay-st-payment). A *fact* is a Checkout session state that `StripeCheckoutService` reads back from Stripe; it arrives by webhook. A *recovery* is the operator's command, which reads the session again and settles it through the same `settle` path. `CheckoutPayment.succeed()` has no guard of its own: the guards on the arrows are checked in `settle` (@fig-a-pay-ds-settle). One rule holds in every state and is not drawn: a fact whose session does not match the saved payment opens the review case `PROVIDER_MISMATCH` and changes no state (A12). No final state is drawn, because a `SUCCEEDED` payment still absorbs repeated and unpaid facts. The outcome (`FULFILLED` or `REFUND_REQUIRED`) is a separate, write-once column set on entry to `SUCCEEDED`, not a state. The operator's commands are tools, not flows of this use case, and are not drawn as sequences: `StripeCheckoutService.recover` (the recovery arrows, which re-read the session and call the same `settle`), `assignReview` and `expireDueSessions`, all started as a command-line option of the application (`--payment-command`, read by `StripeOperationsConfiguration`), never over HTTP; the `CREATE_UNKNOWN` review comes from `prepare` (@fig-a-pay-ds-bind). The purchase intent's lifecycle, including its lapse, is in @sec-a-uc03, and the refund obligation's in @sec-a-uc06.

#afig("a-pay-st-payment", orient: "portrait", [UML statechart of the checkout payment (`CheckoutPayment.Status`). A late payment on a `FAILED` or `CANCELED` attempt is never settled automatically: it opens a review case, and only the operator's recovery moves it to `SUCCEEDED`.])

=== Patterns Applied <sec-a-pay-patterns>

Four patterns are in the Pay upfront code; @tbl-a-pay-patterns maps their roles to classes. Only Adapter is a GoF pattern; the other three are architectural or enterprise integration patterns. The Transactional Outbox that carries `PaymentSucceeded` onwards is in @sec-a-notifications.

#atable(
  [Patterns in the Pay upfront design: the problem first, then the roles mapped to real classes, then the consequences.],
  <tbl-a-pay-patterns>,
  columns: (0.7fr, 1fr, 1.4fr, 1.1fr),
  header: ([Pattern (kind)], [Problem in Pay upfront], [Roles → classes], [Consequences]),
  [Adapter (GoF, structural), as the core of an Anti-Corruption Layer (architectural)], [The payment module needs "create, read and verify a hosted payment" in its own terms, but Stripe's SDK speaks in its own model (`com.stripe.model.checkout.Session`, `StripeException`), which would leak into the application and the domain.], [Target: `CheckoutGateway`. Adapter: `StripeCheckoutGateway`. Adaptee: the Stripe client from `StripeClientProvider`. Translated model: `CheckoutGateway.Session`, `CheckoutGateway.Event`; SDK failures become `PAYMENT_PROVIDER_ERROR`. Client: `StripeCheckoutService` (@fig-a-pay-dc-gateway, @fig-a-pay-ds-bind).], [A second provider is a second adapter; tests use a fake gateway. Liability: the translated records repeat Stripe's vocabulary (`paymentStatus`, `liveMode`), so a Stripe model change still reaches the adapter and its records.],
  [Idempotent Receiver (enterprise integration pattern)], [Stripe delivers each webhook at least once, may deliver several events for one payment, and the buyer may press "Pay" twice.], [Receiver: `StripeCheckoutService`. Message keys: the Stripe event id in `CheckoutPaymentRepository.claimEvent` (`log_payment_webhook_event`); the write-once outcome read by `outcome`; the buyer's `Idempotency-Key` in `findRequest`; the provider key `providerKey()` towards Stripe (@fig-a-pay-ds-settle, @fig-a-pay-ds-outcome).], [A repeat changes nothing and returns the first result (A3, A11; NFR-21, NFR-27). Liability: the claim is written in the settlement transaction, so a rolled-back settlement is retried only when Stripe redelivers (E1).],
  [Ports and Adapters (architectural), with dependency inversion], [Payment must settle money without knowing what it buys (a new team or a seat), and the order module must not reach into payment's internals.], [Ports owned by payment: `PaymentBusinessPort`, `PaymentIntentPort`, `CheckoutGateway`. Adapters: `OrderFulfilmentAdapter` and `PurchaseIntentAppService` (order module, wired in `PaymentIntegrationConfiguration`), `StripeCheckoutGateway` (@fig-a-pay-dc-settle, @fig-a-pay-dc-gateway).], [Payment depends only on interfaces it owns; a third purchase kind is a change in the order module only (ADR 0001). Liabilities: the port's contract (lock order, `MANDATORY` transaction, "technical failures throw, business refusals return") lives in Javadoc, not in the type; and one dependency runs the other way without a port: the order module's intent sweep holds payment's concrete `PaymentIntentExpirySupport` (a read-only service, @fig-a-uc03-dc-expiry).],
  [Publish–subscribe through an in-process event channel (architectural; GoF Observer realised by Spring)], [The integration record for notification-svc must be written with the payment, but the payment module must not depend on the outbox.], [Publisher: `StripeCheckoutService`. Channel: `ApplicationEventPublisher` (Spring's multicaster holds the subscribers). Event: `PaymentSucceeded`. Subscriber: `IntegrationOutboxWriter.onPaymentSucceeded`, `BEFORE_COMMIT` (@sec-a-notifications).], [A new consequence of a payment is a new subscriber. Liability: the event is published before the expiry check, so a refunded payment also yields `PaymentSucceededV1`; consumers must read it as "money received", not "purchase fulfilled".],
)

